  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have
    learned in this lesson.</emphasis></para>
<para>For numbers 1–9, use the following scenario:
  A car has a 16-gallon fuel tank. When driven on a highway, it has a gas mileage of 30 miles per gallon. The gas
  mileage (also called "fuel efficiency") tells us the number of miles the car can travel for a particular amount of
  fuel (one gallon of gasoline, in this case). After filling the gas tank, the driver got on a highway and drove for a
  while.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>How many miles has the car traveled if it has 15 gallons left in the tank?
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 30</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>How many miles has the car traveled if it has 10 gallons left in the tank?
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 180</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>How many miles has the car traveled if it has 2.5 gallons left in the tank?
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 405</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the equation that represents the relationship between the distance the car has traveled in miles, \(d\),
    and the amount of gas left in the tank in gallons, \(x\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(d=16(30-x)\)</item>
  <item>\(x=\frac{d-480}{-30}\)</item>
  <item>\(d=30(16-x)\)</item>
  <item>\(x=\frac{30}{d-480}\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(d=30(16-x)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>How many gallons are left in the tank when the car has traveled 90 miles?
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 13</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>How many gallons are left in the tank when the car has traveled 246 miles?
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 7.8</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose an equation
    that makes it easier to find the amount of gas left in the tank, \(x\), if we know the car has traveled \(d\) miles.
    
  </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(d=16(30-x)\)</item>
  <item>\(x=\frac{d-480}{-30}\)</item>
  <item>\(d=30(16-x)\)</item>
  <item>\(x=\frac{30}{d-480}\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=\frac{d-480}{-30}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Noah was helping to collect the entry fees at his school's football game. Student entry costs $2.75 each and adult
    entry costs $5.25 each. By the end of the game, Diego had collected $281.25.
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Select two </emphasis>equations that could represent the relationship between the number of students, \(s\), the number of adults, \(a\), and the dollar amount collected at the game.
</para>
<list>
  <item>\( 281.25 - 5.25a=2.75s \)</item>
  <item>\( a=53.57- \frac{2.75}{5.25}s \) (<emphasis><emphasis>Note:</emphasis> 53.57 is rounded;
    it’s </emphasis><emphasis>\(\frac{281.25}{5.25}\).</emphasis>)</item>
  <item>\( 281.25-5.25s=a \)</item>
  <item>\( 281.25+2.75a=s \)</item>
  <item>\( 281.25+5.25s=a \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \( 281.25 - 5.25a=2.75s \) and \(a=53.57- \frac{2.75}{5.25}s \)</para>

</section>
